---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";
import { contactEmail } from "../config";

const email = contactEmail();
---
<Prose title="Privacy">
  <p class="mt-4">
    Road to Sixty is run by <a href="https://jordanburch.dev">Jordan Burch</a>. This page says what it keeps about you, where, and how to have it
    removed. In short: only what syncing needs, nothing public, nothing sold.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>The addon on its own</h2>
    <p>The addon saves everything in your own World of Warcraft folder. Nothing leaves your computer unless you install the app and link it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the app sends</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your BattleTag, to show which account a PC is linked to.</li>
      <li>Your Battle.net account number, scrambled with a secret key before it is stored, so the number itself is never kept.</li>
      <li>Each character's name, realm and journey: where it went, quests, kills, levels and gear.</li>
      <li>
        The players each character grouped with: their name, race, class, level and guild, where and when you first met, and the time and dungeons
        you shared.
      </li>
      <li>For each linked PC: the name you gave it, whether it's a Mac or Windows, and its app and addon versions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where it's kept</h2>
    <p>Accounts and the list of versions are in a database on a server Jordan runs. Journey files are in Cloudflare R2 storage. Older versions of a journey are thinned out: every version from the last 30 days is kept, then one a week. Database backups are deleted after 30 days.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Who can see it</h2>
    <p>
      You, through the app on a PC you linked. Jordan runs the server and the storage, so the files can be reached to keep the service working, but
      they aren't looked at otherwise. Nothing is shown publicly, and nothing is sold or shared with anyone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Deleting it</h2>
    <p>
      In the app, open Account and choose Delete everything. Your account and every journey are removed from the server at once, and from backups
      within 30 days. Journeys on your own PCs stay. You can download each character's latest journey first, from the same screen.
    </p>
    <p>You can also ask by email at <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>, for a copy, a correction or deletion.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>This site sets none. The sign-in page on the sync server sets one when you link a PC. Only that page uses it, and it expires within a week.</p>
    <p>
      The site counts visits with Vercel Web Analytics: which pages were viewed, and the country, browser and kind of device. It sets no cookies,
      and it can't tell who you are or follow you to other sites.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site is hosted by Vercel and the downloads by Cloudflare. Like any web host, they see your IP address and browser when you visit, and keep
      their own logs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Battle.net</h2>
    <p>
      Signing in goes through Blizzard's Battle.net. Road to Sixty only receives your account number and BattleTag, never your password. Road to
      Sixty isn't affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.
    </p>
  </section>
</Prose>
